{%- comment -%}
  Bassface size chart — the measurements table inside the PDP's Size Chart row.

  A leaf snippet. It renders the chart title and the table, nothing else: the
  accordion owns the row header, the toggle and the panel padding, so this can
  drop into any container. The consumer loads bf-size-chart.css, same as it does
  for bf-product-card.

  Accepts:
    product - a product object. Required. Renders nothing at all when the
              product resolves to no chart, so the accordion row can test the
              same condition and hide itself rather than open onto emptiness.

  Usage:
    {%- render 'bf-size-chart', product: product -%}

  Data comes from the `size_chart` metaobject, reached through the product's
  `custom.size_chart` reference. Both were built by the scripts in
  new-design/script/ — create-size-chart-entries.mjs owns the three entries,
  assign-size-charts.mjs owns the metafield definition and the assignments, and
  verify-size-chart.mjs checks the pair against assets/size-chart/. Change the
  measurements there, never here.

  size_columns and measurement_rows are list.single_line_text_field, so Liquid
  hands back real arrays. Each measurement row is pipe-delimited, label first:
  "Chest|40|42|44|46|48|50".
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign chart = product.metafields.custom.size_chart.value

  comment
    Fallback by product type, for a product created without the metafield.

    All 92 products carry the reference today, so this never fires for them —
    it is here for product 93, where the alternative is a tee silently
    advertising no sizing at all. The metafield is checked first, so an
    explicit assignment always wins and this costs nothing when it is present.

    "Regular T-Shirt" maps to the STANDARD chart: the product type and the
    chart title disagree on the word, which is exactly why the map is written
    out instead of handleizing product.type — that would miss all 48 of them.
    Same map as assign-size-charts.mjs; change both together.
  endcomment

  unless chart
    case product.type
      when 'Oversized T-Shirt'
        assign fallback = 'unisex-oversized-t-shirt'
      when 'Regular T-Shirt'
        assign fallback = 'unisex-standard-t-shirt'
      when 'Polo T-Shirt'
        assign fallback = 'polo-t-shirt'
    endcase

    if fallback
      assign chart = shop.metaobjects.size_chart[fallback]
    endif
  endunless
-%}

{%- if chart -%}
  {%- assign columns = chart.size_columns.value -%}

  <div class="size-chart">
    <p class="size-chart__title">{{- chart.chart_title.value }} — measurements in {{ chart.unit_label.value -}}</p>

    <div class="size-chart__frame">
      <table class="size-chart__table">
        <thead>
          <tr class="size-chart__row size-chart__row--head">
            <th class="size-chart__cell size-chart__cell--label" scope="col">Size</th>
            {%- for column in columns -%}
              <th class="size-chart__cell" scope="col">{{ column }}</th>
            {%- endfor -%}
          </tr>
        </thead>

        <tbody>
          {%- for row in chart.measurement_rows.value -%}
            {%- assign cells = row | split: '|' -%}
            <tr class="size-chart__row">
              <th class="size-chart__cell size-chart__cell--label" scope="row">{{ cells[0] }}</th>
              {%- comment -%}
                Walked by column rather than by cell, so a row with too few or
                too many values still lands on the header's grid instead of
                shearing the table sideways.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- for column in columns -%}
                <td class="size-chart__cell">{{ cells[forloop.index] }}</td>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </tr>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
